---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import VigenereLab from "../../components/guides/VigenereLab";
import { keyLengths, vigenere } from "../../utils/ciphers";
import { PRACTICE } from "../../utils/samples";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const scores = keyLengths(vigenere(PRACTICE, "MAP", false));
const small = vigenere("THECATSATTHECATRANTHECATHID", "MAP", false);
---

<BaseLayout title="Understand and break Vigenère">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 03 / FOLLOW THE KEY</p>
    <h1>
      One message.
      <br />
      Several shifts<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Build a Vigenère cipher, discover its repeating key, then break an original practice message
      one shift at a time.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <a href="#shifts">01 / Multiple Caesar shifts</a>
      <a href="#detect">02 / Detect the pattern</a>
      <a href="#crib">03 / Try a crib</a>
      <a href="#kasiski">04 / Follow repeated groups</a>
      <a href="#ic">05 / Compare coincidence</a>
      <a href="#solve">06 / Solve the streams</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <h2 id="shifts">A word tells you which shifts to use</h2>
      <p>
        A Caesar cipher uses one shift for the whole message. Vigenère repeats a sequence of shifts.
        Number the alphabet A=0, B=1, …, Z=25. The word <strong>MAP</strong> encodes shifts{" "}
        <strong>12, 0, 15</strong>. Repeat those three shifts: letter 1 uses M, letter 2 uses A,
        letter 3 uses P, then letter 4 uses M again.
      </p>
      <p>
        To encode, add the shift and wrap past Z to A. To decode, subtract it. M (12) + M (12) = Y
        (24). E (4) + A (0) = E (4). E (4) + P (15) = T (19). So the word “meet” begins{" "}
        <strong>YET</strong> when encoded with MAP. The two e’s became different letters because
        they used different key positions.
      </p>
      <VigenereLab part="play" client:load />
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <strong>Try it:</strong> encode MEET with MAP. Then change the key to M: the whole message
        now uses one Caesar shift. Try MAPMAP: six key letters, but the same three shifts repeat, so
        its shortest period is still 3.
      </aside>
      <h2 id="detect">When should you suspect Vigenère?</h2>
      <p>
        One shared shift fails to read the message. Relabelling the largest letter peaks does not
        consistently match English either. Familiar bigrams and trigrams may be spread among several
        encodings, and whole-message letter frequencies often look flatter. A repeated-key
        polyalphabetic cipher such as Vigenère is then worth testing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use these clues together. A short message, an unusual topic or a mixed cipher can look
        similar. <a href={`${base}/guides/identify/`}>Compare the four cipher patterns</a> before
        choosing a method.
      </p>
      <h2 id="crib">Method 1: cribbing</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>crib</strong> is a word or phrase you think might appear. A greeting, a place name
        or a repeated phrase from the puzzle’s story can help. Align the guess with the ciphertext
        and subtract the guessed plaintext values. Those differences are possible key letters.
      </p>
      <VigenereLab part="crib" client:load />
      <p>
        A crib longer than your guessed key length can test the guess: two letters using the same
        key position must demand the same shift. A shorter crib can fit by accident. Keep the
        candidate only if it reads other parts of the message too.
      </p>
      <h2 id="kasiski">Method 2: Kasiski examination</h2>
      <p>
        If a repeated plaintext group meets the same part of a repeating key, it encrypts the same
        way. Count the gaps between the <em>starts</em> of repeated ciphertext groups. A key length
        may divide several of those gaps.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is a deliberately small example. Encode{" "}
        <code>THE CAT SAT THE CAT RAN THE CAT HID</code> with MAP, ignoring spaces. You get{" "}
        <code>{small}</code>. <strong>FHT</strong> starts at letters <strong>1, 10 and 19</strong>.
        The consecutive gaps are <strong>9 and 9</strong>. Both 3 and 9 divide those gaps, so both
        are candidates. The repeated phrase is too regular to decide by itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        The analysis below shows actual start positions, gaps and factor votes. Try groups of 4 or 5
        letters for fewer accidental repeats. Overlapping groups can describe the same underlying
        repetition, so their votes are not independent proof. No repeated group does not rule out
        Vigenère.
      </p>
      <h2 id="ic">Method 3: Index of Coincidence</h2>
      <p>
        Imagine choosing two different positions at random. IC is the probability that their letters
        match. English uses some letters much more often than others, so matches are more common
        than in uniform random letters. Typical reference values are about{" "}
        <strong>0.066 for English</strong> and <strong>1/26 ≈ 0.0385</strong> for uniform A–Z.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>See a small IC calculation</summary>
        <p>
          For AAB there are six ordered ways to pick two different positions. Two choices give the
          two A’s, so IC = 2/6 ≈ 0.3333. For a whole message, add count × (count − 1) for each
          letter, then divide by N × (N − 1), where N is the number of letters. Tiny samples can be
          far from the English reference.
        </p>
      </details>
      <p>
        Try a candidate length. For length 3, put positions 1,4,7,… in stream 1; 2,5,8,… in stream
        2; and 3,6,9,… in stream 3. If the length is right, each stream uses just one Caesar shift,
        which preserves IC. Average the stream IC values and compare several candidate lengths.
      </p>
      <p>
        In our original worked example, length 1 scores <strong>{scores[0].ic.toFixed(4)}</strong>,
        length 3 scores <strong>{scores[2].ic.toFixed(4)}</strong>, and length 6 scores{" "}
        <strong>{scores[5].ic.toFixed(4)}</strong>. Both 3 and its multiple 6 are promising. Use the
        shorter period first and confirm by reading the message. For long keys there may be too few
        letters in each stream to decide.
      </p>
      <h2 id="solve">Put the clues together, then solve the streams</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Compare the Kasiski factors and IC peaks. Try length 3 below.</li>
        <li>
          Select key position 1 and adjust its Caesar shift until the letter frequencies and partial
          message look plausible. The shift suggestions can help.
        </li>
        <li>Repeat for key positions 2 and 3. Check the whole passage after every change.</li>
        <li>
          Use the reveal only after you have tested your idea. If the result is nonsense, revisit
          your length and crib guesses.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <VigenereLab part="break" client:load />
      <p>
        The same analysis works on your own ciphertext in the workbench. Choose Vigenère, compare
        key lengths, then use each key position’s shift control and frequency chart. A readable
        opening is useful; a readable whole message is stronger evidence.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#vigenere`}>
          Open Vigenère tools ↗
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        Examples, calculations and ciphertexts were created for this guide. Method references:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.cs.trincoll.edu/cryptography/vigenere.html">
          Trinity College’s Vigenère tutorial
        </a>{" "}
        (repeating alphabets and separating streams),{" "}
        <a href="https://cs.uri.edu/cryptography/classical-ciphers/vigenere/cryptanalysis-article.html">
          University of Rhode Island’s cryptanalysis notes
        </a>{" "}
        (Kasiski and IC). English group reference ranks elsewhere on this site:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.norvig.com/mayzner.html">Peter Norvig</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
